  <h2 id="the-problem">The Problem: Three Fears Every Beginner Has (and Pricing Pages Make Worse)</h2>
  <p>
    Spend one evening in beginner email-marketing communities and the same three posts repeat:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>1. "I have zero subscribers and I've spent a week comparing platforms."</strong> The choice feels permanent, so it gets over-researched. Plans get renamed, prices are quoted at different list sizes, and the feature that matters most — automation — is quietly paywalled on some tools and free on others. The real cost of choosing wrong is not the subscription. It is migration: rebuilding every form, landing page and welcome sequence in a new tool, three months in, when you should be publishing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2. "Will my emails even reach the inbox?"</strong> New senders are haunted by the spam folder. Authentication settings (SPF, DKIM, DMARC), shared sending reputation on free plans, account-approval reviews — beginners cannot tell which of these are real risks and which are noise, and pricing pages do not help. The honest answer: all four tools here are established senders with solid infrastructure. What lands a beginner in spam is almost never the logo on the tool — it is a cold or purchased list, no domain authentication, and sending volume spikes. Any of these four beats any of these four plus a dirty list.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>3. "Am I paying for subscribers I don't have?"</strong> Per-subscriber billing sounds fair until you read the counting rules. Some platforms bill for unsubscribed and bounced contacts. Prices step up the moment your list grows — success is taxed automatically, sometimes with an automatic card charge. And free plans have cliffs: cross the cap by one subscriber and sending locks until you upgrade or delete people. This is where beginners get hurt, and it is where this comparison spends most of its attention.
  </p>

