  <h2 id="pricing-tool-by-tool">Pricing, Tool by Tool — With the Real Limits</h2>

  <h3><a href={kitUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kit</a> — free for a long time, then a real jump</h3>
  <p>
    Kit's free plan is the most generous runway here: <strong>up to 10,000 subscribers</strong>, unlimited email sends, unlimited landing pages and forms, one user. What it withholds is the engine room — <strong>no automations and no email sequences on Free</strong>. The moment you want a welcome sequence (the first thing most creators actually need), you are on Creator: <strong>$39/mo billed monthly, or $390/year (about $33/mo) at 1,000 subscribers</strong>, rising with list size (at 10,000 subscribers Creator is $139/mo billed monthly). Pro ($79/mo at 1,000) adds engagement scoring, deliverability reporting and a newsletter referral system. Selling through Kit costs <strong>3.5% + 30¢ per sale on every plan</strong>, card processing included. Paid plans include a free white-glove migration from your old platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: when your list crosses a tier, Kit <strong>automatically upgrades you</strong> — no sending interruption, but no asking first either. Cleaning your list back down does <strong>not</strong> auto-downgrade; you have to email support. There is a 30-day refund window from signup.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={beehiivUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow">Beehiiv</a> — free to publish, $49 to monetise</h3>
  <p>
    Beehiiv's free plan covers <strong>up to 2,500 subscribers with unlimited sends</strong>, a newsletter website, one publication, one seat, and access to its recommendation network — genuinely enough to start and grow a newsletter. The wall is money features: <strong>paid subscriptions, the ad network and automations start on Lite at $49/mo billed yearly ($59/mo monthly)</strong> at 1,000 subscribers, and removing beehiiv branding requires <strong>Pro at $95/mo billed yearly ($109 monthly)</strong>. On paid plans beehiiv takes <strong>0% of your paid subscription and product revenue</strong> — you keep everything except Stripe's standard 2.9% + $0.30.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: beehiiv <strong>renamed and repriced every plan on October 1, 2026</strong> (Launch → Free, Scale → Lite, Max → Pro) and raised paid prices at every tier — any review written before October 2026 is quoting dead prices, including beehiiv's own older blog posts. Lite's price also climbs steeply with list size ($119/mo billed yearly at 10,000 subscribers).
  </p>

  <h3><a href={mailerLiteUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MailerLite</a> — the cheapest real start, with the smallest free room</h3>
  <p>
    MailerLite is the only tool here with <strong>automations on the free plan</strong>, and its paid step is the cheapest in this comparison: <strong>Comfort from $12/mo at 500 subscribers ($10.80/mo billed yearly — a flat 10% annual discount)</strong>. Comfort includes 3 seats and monthly sends of 10× your subscriber tier. <strong>Power, from $25/mo ($22.50 yearly)</strong>, removes the ceilings: unlimited sends, seats and automations, plus 24/7 live chat support.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: the free plan shrank on <strong>June 16, 2026 — from 500 to 250 subscribers and from 12,000 to 2,500 emails a month</strong> (older reviews still quote the old limits). Pass 250 active subscribers on Free and <strong>sending locks</strong> for campaigns and automations until you upgrade or trim the list. MailerLite counts subscribers <strong>cumulatively within a billing cycle</strong>: someone you delete mid-cycle still counts until your billing date. On paid plans, crossing your tier <strong>automatically charges your card</strong> for the next one — convenient, and worth knowing before it happens.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={activeCampaignUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ActiveCampaign</a> — no free plan, and read the counting rules</h3>
  <p>
    ActiveCampaign is the automation heavyweight — and the only tool here with <strong>no free plan at all</strong>, just a 14-day trial. <strong>Starter is $15/mo billed yearly ($19/mo month-to-month) at 1,000 contacts</strong>, with sends capped at 10× your contact count, 1 user, and automations limited to <strong>5 actions each</strong>. Plus ($49/mo yearly) unlocks landing pages, the built-in CRM, lead scoring and more automation depth; Pro ($79/mo yearly) adds predictive sending and conditional content.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing — this is the section to read twice: accounts created on or after <strong>November 3, 2025 are billed for every contact in the account, including unsubscribed, bounced and unconfirmed contacts</strong>. You pay for people you cannot email. Prices also climb fast with list size: Starter is $149/mo at 10,000 contacts. None of this makes ActiveCampaign a bad tool — it makes it a tool to buy deliberately, when automation complexity is already your bottleneck, not a first experiment.
  </p>

  <h3>The free all-in-one alternative: <a href={systemeAffiliateUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow">Systeme.io</a></h3>
  <p>
    If your plan is "newsletter <strong>and</strong> a course <strong>and</strong> checkout", none of the four above bundles that — you would be stacking tools again. Systeme.io's free plan covers <strong>2,000 contacts with unlimited sends</strong>, plus funnels, one online course and checkout at <strong>0% platform transaction fees</strong>, in one dashboard. Its email automation is basic (one automation rule on Free) next to Kit or ActiveCampaign — that trade is the whole story. Full breakdown in our <a href="/blog/systeme-io-review">Systeme.io review</a>.
  </p>

